  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find the first two terms before summing",
      "body": "<p>An even integer power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is 1; an odd integer power is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> into all three parts of the term.</p>",
      "recap": "The parity of the exponent controls a power of minus one.",
      "pitfall": "Keep the subtraction sign outside the second power: subtracting minus one adds one.",
      "pause": "Write the first four terms and look for a repeated block.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "Write initial terms and use the sequence rule to identify whether a repeating block occurs."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Group complete repetitions",
      "body": "Once you have simplified the first few terms, compare terms with the same parity. Use this to establish a repeating block.",
      "recap": "A periodic sum consists of complete blocks followed by any remaining terms.",
      "pitfall": "Check the starting sign and the inclusive number of terms before grouping.",
      "pause": "Find the block sum, count the complete blocks and evaluate any remainder.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b3-l09",
          "reminder": "A periodic sum consists of complete blocks followed by any remaining terms."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Simplify one term.</strong> The exponents n and n+2 have the same parity, while n-1 has the opposite parity. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Pair adjacent terms.</strong> The sequence is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x02026;</mi></mrow></math>. Each pair has sum 0, and the first 38 terms give 19 such pairs.</p><p><strong>3. Add the unpaired term.</strong> The 39th term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so the complete sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>19</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer B.</strong></p>",
